#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5e58b3-f6d7-56e2-bccd-97095e94495e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Three Variable Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "7bc66df5-26b7-5eb0-b756-f8b086607209" :question/math-academy-id "e-1520" :question/problem "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $9a$, $18a,$ and $27a.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $9$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $18$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 3,\\quad 6,\\quad 9,\\quad 18.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $27$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9,\\quad 27.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $9,$ $18,$ and $27$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 9.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $9.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. The expressions $9a,$ $18a,$ and $27a$ all contain $a.$\n\nThus, the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $9a,$ $18a,$ and $27a$ is $9a.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "872ca3cb-830c-5af5-92f1-230611a8a693" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when there are No Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "aa1c4cbd-2086-5634-809f-43e4dd6becf9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1603" :question/problem "What is the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $4a$ and $15b$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n15b &= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we try to pair off the individual factors, we can't find any common factors. The only common factor is one. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(4a, 15b) = 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92176bb0-84dc-5fa4-8154-e974c05fff06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of a Number and a Variable Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "6ce77198-a3b2-5e9b-98d5-db8922addc1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-816" :question/problem "What is $\\textrm{GCF}(10k,20)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $10$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 5,\\quad 10.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $20$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 4,\\quad 5,\\quad 10,\\quad 20.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $10$ and $20$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 5,\\quad 10.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $10.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since $20$ has no variable and $10k$ contains $k,$ there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, $\\textrm{GCF}(10k,20)=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ac1e8c-128c-5c13-b845-105092ae74d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Two Variable Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "f4d78520-1050-5ca1-a2da-1b813269dd9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-817" :question/problem "Find the greatest common factor of the terms $33p$ and $22p.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $33$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 11,\\quad 33.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $22$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 2,\\quad 11,\\quad 22.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $33$ and $22$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 11.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $11.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Both $33p$ and $22p$ contain $p.$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $33p$ and $22p$ is $11p.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ac1e8c-128c-5c13-b845-105092ae74d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Two Variable Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455812b7-5521-5f6b-94b1-0053a2a496ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458754} {:answer/id #uuid "d0208325-8735-55d5-890a-2275fd465c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458988} {:answer/id #uuid "e01dd02b-fcd7-50e8-ae50-615cf2d0e2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459015} {:answer/id #uuid "e2be7b4a-ffd9-5b43-b43c-a009d111c8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459023} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd29902-cd31-5bec-8400-04e7a171e358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e01dd02b-fcd7-50e8-ae50-615cf2d0e2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459015} :answer-field/id #uuid "140a739a-1031-560e-aa4d-90416f54ab6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "184fbbe9-0f41-564a-b670-f07cc1604886" :question/math-academy-id "q-359041" :question/problem "What is the $\\text{GCF}$ of $4x$ and $2x?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $4$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,4.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $2$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $4$ and $2$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $2.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Both $4x$ and $2x$ contain $x.$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $4x$ and $2x$ is $2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92176bb0-84dc-5fa4-8154-e974c05fff06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of a Number and a Variable Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070fb2ab-1a23-5f09-8c6c-10da5c8814e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458628} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcc95c4-ceea-54bb-bf0e-5aea37101b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458717} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd21e0f-ffa7-5990-b0b1-e1f3eed75fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458851} {:answer/id #uuid "bc09f845-76ee-5f3d-b378-7930ae2f283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458968} {:answer/id #uuid "d827cefa-7f4a-53cd-a6d9-b1fe21587fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d827cefa-7f4a-53cd-a6d9-b1fe21587fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3533f96d-a180-5fd8-b0fa-542038373ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b234a80-5e71-5a68-b1f2-e9e3690c0639" :question/math-academy-id "q-359045" :question/problem "What is the greatest common factor of $16$ and $24t?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and identify any common variable factors in the expressions.\n\nThe factors of $16$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,4,\\,8,\\,16.\n$$\n\nThe factors of $24$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,12,\\,24.\n$$\n\nThe common factors of $16$ and $24$ are\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,4,\\,8.\n$$\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is $8.$\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since $16$ has no variable and $24t$ contains $t,$ there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $16$ and $24t$ is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ac1e8c-128c-5c13-b845-105092ae74d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Two Variable Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1467f0d7-22c6-5a6f-bc0c-80d825d4ebba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458660} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdc7fb7-7795-5239-a7c0-3e42a25a6999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458747} {:answer/id #uuid "6754cddc-8eba-52ba-92fe-a5d36ffde22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458810} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1ca50b-23ee-572f-8c1b-8a27c032cfae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458850} {:answer/id #uuid "a10eb717-7fba-580c-9671-529cc7bd5192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/i